@@ -0,0 +1,20 @@
+# 0001 — Reservation expiry is evaluated lazily from the clock
+
+Status: accepted (2026-10-29)
+
+## Context
+Holds that nobody confirms must lapse after a TTL. The package has no I/O and no
+scheduler; all time comes from the injected `Clock`.
+
+## Decision
+There is no sweep. A reservation is expired when its stored status is `ACTIVE`
+and `clock.now() >= expires_at`. Every read path (availability, lookup,
+confirm, release, idempotent replay) applies this rule; the stored status is
+updated to `EXPIRED` when such a reservation is touched, but correctness never
+depends on that write having happened.
+
+## Consequences
+- Any new code that counts held units must use the effective (clock-evaluated)
+  status, not the stored one.
+- A database-backed repository must keep this rule (e.g. filter on
+  `expires_at > now` in queries) rather than relying on a job.
