+++ b/specs/stock-reservations.md
@@ -0,0 +1,105 @@
+# Stock reservations
+
+## What and why
+
+Checkout needs to hold stock for a cart before payment, then either confirm the
+hold into an order or release it. Abandoned carts must not lock stock forever,
+so a hold nobody confirms lapses on its own after 20 minutes. The cart service
+retries requests, so creating a hold is idempotent on a caller-supplied key.
+
+A **reservation** (a "hold") takes units out of *available* without touching
+the ledger or *on-hand*. Confirming it turns the held units into an ordinary
+`PLACED` order; releasing or expiry frees them.
+
+## Interface (fixed by the requester — verbatim)
+
+Hidden tests call exactly this; `InventoryService(repository, ledger, clock)` keeps working as is:
+- `InventoryService.reserve(warehouse: str, items: dict[str, int], idempotency_key: str) -> Reservation`
+- `InventoryService.confirm_reservation(reservation_id: str) -> Order`
+- `InventoryService.release_reservation(reservation_id: str) -> Reservation`
+- `InventoryService.get_reservation(reservation_id: str) -> Reservation`
+- `InventoryService.available(sku, warehouse)` must take holds into account.
+- `inventory.models`: dataclass `Reservation` with `reservation_id: str`, `warehouse: str`, `items: dict[str, int]` (units held per SKU), `status: ReservationStatus`, `expires_at: datetime`, `order_id: str | None`; enum `ReservationStatus` with members `ACTIVE`, `CONFIRMED`, `RELEASED`, `EXPIRED`.
+- `inventory.errors`: new `ReservationNotFound`, `ReservationExpired`, `InvalidReservationState`, `IdempotencyConflict` (all subclass `InventoryError`); reuse the existing errors where they fit.
+- Time comes only from the injected clock. There is no background job: expiry must take effect without anyone calling a sweep.
+
+## Acceptance criteria
+
+### Creating a hold
+- [ ] AC1 `reserve` returns a `Reservation` with status `ACTIVE`, `order_id is None`,
+      and `expires_at == clock.now() + 20 minutes` at creation.
+- [ ] AC2 The TTL defaults to 20 minutes; it may be overridden by an optional
+      constructor keyword. `InventoryService(repository, ledger, clock)` still works.
+- [ ] AC3 Partial holds: each line holds `min(requested, available now)`. A line
+      with nothing available is absent from `Reservation.items` (no zero entries).
+- [ ] AC4 If no line can hold anything, `InsufficientStock` is raised and no
+      reservation is created.
+- [ ] AC5 Bad input rejects the whole request and holds nothing, even for valid
+      lines: unknown warehouse → `UnknownWarehouse`, unknown SKU → `UnknownSku`,
+      zero/negative/non-integer quantity → `InvalidQuantity`. Empty `items` raises
+      an `InventoryError`.
+- [ ] AC6 Reservation ids are unique.
+
+### Availability
+- [ ] AC7 `available(sku, wh) = max(0, on_hand − allocated to PLACED orders −
+      held by ACTIVE, unexpired reservations)`, per SKU per warehouse.
+- [ ] AC8 A hold in one warehouse does not change availability in another.
+- [ ] AC9 Holds never write to the ledger and never change `on_hand`.
+- [ ] AC10 `receive` and `adjust` are never blocked by holds. A write-off that
+      leaves on-hand below what is held makes `available` 0; the reservations
+      are unchanged.
+- [ ] AC11 `place_order` cannot take held units and stays all-or-nothing.
+
+### Expiry
+- [ ] AC12 From `expires_at` on (that instant included) an `ACTIVE` hold is
+      expired: its units are available again and `get_reservation` reports
+      status `EXPIRED`. No sweep call is needed.
+- [ ] AC13 Only `ACTIVE` holds expire; `CONFIRMED` and `RELEASED` never change on
+      their own. Expired reservations stay retrievable.
+
+### Confirm
+- [ ] AC14 Confirming an active hold creates an ordinary `PLACED` order in the
+      same warehouse for exactly the held units (after a partial hold, only
+      those). The reservation becomes `CONFIRMED` with `order_id` set.
+- [ ] AC15 `available` is the same just before and just after confirm; `on_hand`
+      moves only when the order ships.
+- [ ] AC16 Confirming an already `CONFIRMED` reservation returns the same order
+      and creates nothing new — at any time, even after 20 minutes have passed.
+- [ ] AC17 Confirming an expired hold (even one that just lapsed) raises
+      `ReservationExpired`.
+- [ ] AC18 Confirming a `RELEASED` reservation raises `InvalidReservationState`.
+
+### Release
+- [ ] AC19 Releasing an `ACTIVE` hold sets status `RELEASED` and frees its units.
+- [ ] AC20 Releasing a `RELEASED` or `EXPIRED` reservation is a no-op that
+      returns it unchanged.
+- [ ] AC21 Releasing a `CONFIRMED` reservation raises `InvalidReservationState`.
+
+### Lookup
+- [ ] AC22 `get_reservation`, `confirm_reservation` and `release_reservation`
+      raise `ReservationNotFound` for an unknown id.
+
+### Idempotency
+- [ ] AC23 `reserve` with a key already used for the same warehouse and the same
+      *requested* items returns the existing reservation in its current state:
+      no new hold, `expires_at` unchanged — even if it is finished
+      (confirmed, released, expired).
+- [ ] AC24 A key already used with a different warehouse or different requested
+      items raises `IdempotencyConflict`, even if the original is finished.
+      Input is validated first: a used key with invalid input raises the
+      validation error (AC5), not `IdempotencyConflict`.
+- [ ] AC25 A `reserve` call that fails (any error) does not consume its key; a
+      retry is evaluated afresh.
+- [ ] AC26 Keys are global (not per warehouse) and remembered for the life of
+      the repository.
+
+## Non-goals
+- No background job or explicit sweep API.
+- `stock_report` is not required to show held units (any treatment is acceptable).
+- Extending or shortening an existing hold; editing its items.
+- Reservation id format.
+- Persisting anything beyond the in-memory repository.
+
+## Scope limits
+- `place_order`, `ship_order`, `cancel_order` behaviour is unchanged.
+- No new dependencies.
